There is no official '''flag for Northern Ireland''' except for the [[Union Flag]] of the [[United Kingdom]] as a whole. No ''de jure'' local flag represents [[Northern Ireland]] specifically.<ref name=minahan>{{cite book |last=Minahan |first=James |year=2009 |title=The Complete Guide to National Symbols and Emblems |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=jfrWCQAAQBAJ |publisher=ABC-CLIO |page=486 |isbn=9780313344978 |quote=The official flag of the province is the Union Flag. There is no official national flag of Northern Ireland, following the Northern Ireland Constitution Act of 1973, nor any unofficial flag universally accepted in Northern Ireland. |archive-date=3 December 2024 |access-date=23 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241203090220/https://books.google.com/books?id=jfrWCQAAQBAJ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=mccormick>{{cite book |last=McCormick |first=John |year=2012 |title=Contemporary Britain |url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=113701718X |publisher=Palgrave Macmillan |page=53 |quote=The old flag of Northern Ireland – a red hand inside a white star on a red cross – has strong connections with the Protestant community, and is no longer official but is still occasionally flown. The official flag of Northern Ireland is the Union Flag.}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=McCartney |first=Clem |year=1994 |title=Clashing Symbols?: A Report on the Use of Flags, Anthems and Other National Symbols in Northern Ireland |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=pIdnAAAAMAAJ |publisher=Queen's University of Belfast |pages=150–151 |isbn=9780853895381 |quote=In December 1986 the [[Northern Ireland Office]] produced an Explanatory Document on the [Flags and Emblems Act], which stated: [...] "Repeal of the Act would make no change whatsoever to the position that the Union flag is the official flag of Northern Ireland as it is of the United Kingdom as a whole'.}}</ref>

The [[Ulster Banner]] was used by the [[Government of Northern Ireland (1921–1972)|Northern Irish government]] from 1953 until the government and [[Parliament of Northern Ireland|parliament]] were [[Northern Ireland Constitution Act 1973|abolished]] in 1973. Since then, it has had no official status.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.flaginstitute.org/wp/flags/ulster/ |title=Ulster |website=[[Flag Institute]] |quote=The Ulster flag is different from the Ulster Banner, which was the former flag of Northern Ireland but now holds no official status. |access-date=22 May 2016 |archive-date=24 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924014050/http://www.flaginstitute.org/wp/flags/ulster/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="nolan-bryan">{{cite book |author1=Paul Nolan |author2=Dominic Bryan |year=2016 |title=Flags: Towards a New Understanding |url=https://www.qub.ac.uk/schools/IrishStudiesGateway/FileStore/Filetoupload,566329,en.pdf |publisher=Queen's University Belfast |page=6 |quote=following the Northern Ireland Constitution Act of 1973, the Ulster Banner ceased to have any official standing, but there followed a huge increase in its unofficial use as a symbol of loyalism. |access-date=30 May 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160430115456/http://www.qub.ac.uk/schools/IrishStudiesGateway/FileStore/Filetoupload,566329,en.pdf |archive-date=30 April 2016 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="sportni">{{Cite report |url=http://www.sportni.net/wp-content/uploads/2013/09/Promotingfairplayinsport.pdf |title=Promoting Fair Play in Sport: A Guide to Sport and the Equality Legislation in Northern Ireland |date=n.d. |publisher=[[Sport Northern Ireland]] |access-date=19 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220305105140/http://www.sportni.net/wp-content/uploads/2013/09/Promotingfairplayinsport.pdf |archive-date=5 March 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="hain">{{cite web |url=https://www.theyworkforyou.com/wrans/?id=2007-05-14b.135038.h |title=Northern Ireland Executive: Flags |date=14 May 2007 |website=[[TheyWorkForYou]] |publisher=[[Hansard]] |quote=[[Peter Hain]], [[Secretary of State for Northern Ireland]]: 'The Ulster flag and the Cross of St. Patrick have no official status and under the Flags Regulations are not permitted to be flown from Government Buildings'. |access-date=18 June 2018 |archive-date=28 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180628015714/https://www.theyworkforyou.com/wrans/?id=2007-05-14b.135038.h |url-status=live }}</ref> However, it is still used as the flag of Northern Ireland by both [[Ulster loyalism|loyalists]] and [[Unionism in Ireland|unionists]]<ref>[http://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/20101103103930/http://www.ofmdfmni.gov.uk/flags-monitoring.pdf Flags Monitoring Project 2006: Preliminary Findings] Queen's University, Belfast. p.25. "The meaning attached by people to these regional flags can vary. This is particularly true of the Northern Ireland or Ulster flag which would have been extensively used by loyalists since 1972. Also, it has no official status as a flag for Northern Ireland."</ref> and to represent Northern Ireland internationally in some sporting competitions, like the [[Commonwealth Games]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Home of the Birmingham 2022 Commonwealth Games |url=https://www.birmingham2022.com/www.birmingham2022.com/ |access-date=17 September 2022 |website=Commonwealth Games - Birmingham 2022 |language=en }}{{Dead link|date=September 2025 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>

The [[Saint Patrick's Saltire]] represents Northern Ireland indirectly as [[Kingdom of Ireland|Ireland]] in the Union Flag. It is sometimes flown during [[Saint Patrick's Day]] parades in Northern Ireland<ref name="Groom2007">{{cite book |last=Groom |first=Nick |author-link=Nick Groom |chapter=Union Jacks and Union Jills |editor-last1=Eriksen |editor-first1=Thomas Hylland |editor-link1=Thomas Hylland Eriksen |editor-last2=Jenkins |editor-first2=Richard |title=Flag, Nation and Symbolism in Europe and America |publisher=Routledge |year=2007 |pages=68–87 |isbn=978-0-415-44404-0 |chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=scHXHTkRmZcC&pg=PA68 |location=Abingdon |lccn=2007018505 |oclc=123968978 |ol=9353071W}}</ref> and is used to represent Northern Ireland during some royal events.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.flaginstitute.org/Thames_Pageant_Flag_Guide.pdf |title=A Visual Guide to the Flags used in the Thames Diamond Jubilee Pageant |last=Bartram |first=Graham |author-link=Graham Bartram |year=2012 |publisher=[[Flag Institute]] |page=5 |access-date=22 August 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121108075308/http://www.flaginstitute.org/Thames_Pageant_Flag_Guide.pdf |archive-date=8 November 2012}}</ref>

The flying of various flags in Northern Ireland is [[Northern Ireland flags issue|a significant sectarian issue]], with different communities identifying with different flags.<ref name=":0" />

Since at least as early as 2013, there have been calls for a new, neutral flag for Northern Ireland,<ref name=kilclooney>{{cite web |url=http://www.newsletter.co.uk/news/northern-ireland-news/new-northern-ireland-flag-should-be-created-says-lord-kilclooney-1-5753950 |title=New Northern Ireland flag should be created, says Lord Kilclooney |date=17 December 2013 |website=[[The News Letter]] |quote=[[John Taylor, Baron Kilclooney|Lord Kilclooney]], the former Ulster Unionist deputy leader, is a vice chairman of Westminsters all-party group on flags and heraldry which promotes the flying of the Union Flag. He told the News Letter […] ‘whilst England (St George’s Cross) Scotland (St Andrew’s Cross) and Wales (The Dragon) have individual regional flags, the Flags Institute in London confirms that Northern Ireland has no official regional flag’. |access-date=22 May 2016 |archive-date=24 June 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160624210228/http://www.newsletter.co.uk/news/northern-ireland-news/new-northern-ireland-flag-should-be-created-says-lord-kilclooney-1-5753950 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/news/northern-ireland/find-a-neutral-flag-for-all-of-northern-ireland-richard-haass-issues-challenge-to-parties-29803598.html |title=Find a neutral flag for all of Northern Ireland: Richard Haass issues challenge to parties |date=3 December 2013 |website=[[Belfast Telegraph]] |access-date=21 June 2016 |archive-date=6 August 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160806174347/http://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/news/northern-ireland/find-a-neutral-flag-for-all-of-northern-ireland-richard-haass-issues-challenge-to-parties-29803598.html |url-status=live }}</ref> including as a recommendation by the Commission on Flags, Identity, Culture and Tradition in December 2021.<ref name="BBC News">{{Cite news |last=McCormack |first=Jayne |date=4 December 2021 |title=Flags report: Five things we learned from 168-page document |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-northern-ireland-59522317 |work=BBC News |archive-date=8 December 2021 |access-date=8 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211208124932/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-northern-ireland-59522317 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==<span class="anchor" id="1924-1973"></span> Flag of the Government of Northern Ireland (1924–1973)==
{{Main|Ulster Banner}}
The Ulster Banner, also known as the [[Red Hand of Ulster|Red Hand]] Flag or the Ulster Flag (not to be confused with the [[Provinces of Ireland|provincial]] [[Flag of Ulster]]), was the flag that was granted a royal warrant for use to the [[Executive Committee of the Privy Council of Northern Ireland|Government of Northern Ireland]] in 1924. In common with other British flags, any civic status of the flag was not defined in law.<ref name=EB>Encyclopedia Britannica says: "According to British tradition, a [[coat of arms]] or flag is granted to the government of a territory, not to the people residing there. Therefore, when the government of Northern Ireland was disbanded in March 1972, its arms and flag officially disappeared; however, the flag continues to be used by groups (such as sports teams) representing the territory in sport."</ref>

The Government of Northern Ireland was [[Coat of arms of Northern Ireland|granted arms]] by [[Warrant (law)|royal warrant]] and had the right to display these arms on a flag or banner. This right was exercised for the [[Coronation of Elizabeth II|coronation of Queen Elizabeth II in 1953]] when the banner was flown for the first time over [[Parliament Buildings (Northern Ireland)|Parliament Buildings]] in honor of Elizabeth's visit. Also during her visit, on 1 July 1953, the Minister for Home Affairs announced that, while the Union Flag was the only standard officially recognized, those who wished to have a distinctive Ulster symbol might use the banner.<ref>McCartney, Clem & Bryon, Lucy. ''Clashing Symbols? A report on using flags, anthems and other national symbols in Northern Ireland.'' The Institute for Irish Studies, Queen's University of Belfast, 1994, p. 42 {{ISBN|085-389-538-4}}</ref> When the [[Parliament of Northern Ireland]] was dissolved by the [[British government]] under the [[Northern Ireland Constitution Act 1973]], the flag ceased to be used by a body with a royal warrant but remains the only flag {{As of|2007|alt=to date}} which represents Northern Ireland at international level in sport.<ref name=EB/>

Since the Northern Ireland government and parliament were abolished in 1972, the use of the Ulster Banner among loyalists has increased.<ref>{{Cite book|title=Our Own Devices: National Symbols and Political Conflict in Twentieth-century Ireland (New Directions History)|last=Morris|first=Ewan|publisher=Irish Academic Press Ltd|year=2005|isbn=978-0716533375|pages=205|quote="The main change to the use of symbols within the unionist community in recent decades has been the growing popularity of the Northern Ireland flag since the early 1970s when it came into widespread use by loyalists who felt that they had been betrayed by the government at Westminster. The increasing use of the Northern Ireland flag has sometimes been seen as symptomatic of a growing sense of Ulster nationality..."}}</ref><ref name=ward>{{cite book|last1=Ward|first1=Paul|title=Britisher Since 1870|date=15 April 2004|publisher=Routledge|isbn=978-0415220170|pages=166|quote=From the early 1970s some unionists have sought increasingly to stress their identity with Ulster [...] Since the 1970s the use of the Northern Ireland flag has become prominent, further emphasising the desire to stress an Ulster identity.}}</ref>

==Official use of flags==
There are various practices for the flying of flags by public bodies in Northern Ireland. The Flags Regulations (Northern Ireland) Order 2000 requires that the Union Flag be flown over specified government buildings including Parliament Buildings and state offices on specified "named days" (for example, it was traditionally used to honour the late [[Elizabeth II|Queen Elizabeth II]]'s official birthday).

The regulations also provide that, on the occasion of a visit to a government building by the [[Monarchy of the United Kingdom|British Monarch]], the [[Royal Standard of the United Kingdom|Royal Standard]] shall be flown, and the Union Flag can be flown, and on state visits from other heads of state the Union Flag and the national flag of the country of the visitor can be flown. The regulations prohibit any flags being flown from the relevant buildings except as expressly permitted by the regulations.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.opsi.gov.uk/sr/sr2000/20000347.htm|title=The Flags Regulations (Northern Ireland) 2000|website=Opsi.gov.uk|access-date=9 January 2018|archive-date=9 December 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091209115902/http://www.opsi.gov.uk/sr/sr2000/20000347.htm|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|date=26 July 2018|title=NI to lose a designated day for flying the Union flag|language=en-GB|work=BBC News|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-northern-ireland-44960653|access-date=2 August 2021|archive-date=2 August 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210802212334/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-northern-ireland-44960653|url-status=live}}</ref>

When flags representing the "Home Countries" of England, Northern Ireland, Scotland and Wales are flown at official ceremonies, Northern Ireland is sometimes represented by the [[Saint Patrick's Saltire|Saint Patrick's Cross]], for instance on the [[Gloriana (barge)|barge ''Gloriana'']] during the 2012 [[Thames Diamond Jubilee Pageant]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.flaginstitute.org/Thames_Pageant_Flag_Guide.pdf|title=A Visual Guide to the Flags used in the Thames Diamond Jubilee Pageant|last=Bartram|first=Graham|year=2012|publisher=The Flag Institute|page=5|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121108075308/http://www.flaginstitute.org/Thames_Pageant_Flag_Guide.pdf#5|archive-date=8 November 2012|access-date=6 April 2016}}</ref> In May 2016, the Ulster Banner was flown from horseback during the Musical Ride of the [[Household Cavalry]] at the Queen's 90th birthday celebration at Windsor, alongside the flags of [[Flag of England|England]], [[Flag of Scotland|Scotland]] and [[Flag of Wales|Wales]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.itv.com/hub/the-queens-90th-birthday-celebration/2a3926a0001|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160519160614/http://www.itv.com/hub/the-queens-90th-birthday-celebration/2a3926a0001|url-status=dead|archive-date=19 May 2016|title=The Queen's 90th Birthday Celebration - Sunday 15 May 8.35pm - The ITV Hub|date=19 May 2016|access-date=9 January 2018}}</ref><ref>Archived at [https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211212/Rjf6kCdB52c Ghostarchive]{{cbignore}} and the [https://web.archive.org/web/20170103193032/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rjf6kCdB52c&t=59m26s Wayback Machine]{{cbignore}}: {{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rjf6kCdB52c&t=59m26s|title=YouTube Mirror|website=[[YouTube]]|date=16 May 2016 |access-date=9 January 2018}}{{cbignore}}</ref><ref>The Queen's 90th Birthday Celebration, Souvenir Programme, Regal Press Ltd, 2016, p. 12, {{ISBN|9781906670405}}</ref>

Other regulations exist for other public bodies in Northern Ireland. Use of flags by the [[Police Service of Northern Ireland]] is governed by the Police Emblems and Flags Regulations (Northern Ireland) 2002, which provides that no flag shall be used by the service except for its own flag.

===Local authorities===
Legislation relating to flag flying does not apply to district council buildings, and district councils follow a range of practices varying from flying the Union Flag on a number of council buildings every day of the year as at [[Lisburn]], to flying no flags on any building, flying only the council flag or flying flags on the designated days in the same way as government buildings.<ref>[http://www.ofmdfmni.gov.uk/flags.pdf Transforming Conflict: Flags and Emblems] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131023154742/http://www.ofmdfmni.gov.uk/flags.pdf |date=23 October 2013 }} by Dominic Bryan and Gordon Gillespie, Institute of Irish Studies, Queen's University, Belfast, March 2005</ref>

In 2004, [[Belfast City Council]] commissioned a study on the flying of the Union Flag which noted that the Ulster Banner was flown alongside it by three unionist-controlled [[local government in Northern Ireland|district councils]] at that time: [[Ards (borough)|Ards]], [[Carrickfergus Borough Council|Carrickfergus]] and [[Castlereagh (borough)|Castlereagh]].<ref>Belfast City Council (May 2004), [http://www.belfastcity.gov.uk/equality/docs/FlyingOfUnionFlagEQIA.pdf Flying of the Union Flag: An Equality Impact Assessment]. {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070615135038/http://www.belfastcity.gov.uk/equality/docs/FlyingOfUnionFlagEQIA.pdf |date=15 June 2007 }}</ref> These councils have since [[Reform of local government in Northern Ireland|been replaced]].

==Displaying flags==
{{Main|Northern Ireland flags issue}}
In Northern Ireland, some members from each of the unionist and nationalist communities use flags to declare their political allegiances and to mark territory.<ref name=":0">Dominic Bryan & Gordon Gillespie (2005) [http://cain.ulst.ac.uk/images/symbols/bryangillespie0305flags.pdf ''Transforming Conflict: Flags and Emblems''] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130615203058/http://cain.ulst.ac.uk/images/symbols/bryangillespie0305flags.pdf |date=15 June 2013 }}, Belfast: Institute of Irish Studies, Queens University Belfast</ref> Unionists and loyalists fly the Union Flag and Ulster Banner to show their support for the union and/or their allegiance to Northern Ireland. Irish nationalists and republicans fly the [[Flag of Ireland|Irish tricolour]] to show their support for a [[United Ireland]].<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20121112023328/http://www.taoiseach.gov.ie/eng/Historical_Information/About_the_Constitution,_Flag,_Anthem_Harp/The_National_Flag-PDF.pdf ''The National Flag''], Department of the Taoiseach</ref>

After the 1998 [[Good Friday Agreement]], flags continue to be a source of disagreement in Northern Ireland. The Agreement states that:

{{blockquote|All participants acknowledge the sensitivity of the use of symbols and emblems for public purposes, and the need in particular in creating the new institutions to ensure that such symbols and emblems are used in a manner which promotes mutual respect rather than division.<ref>[http://peacemaker.un.org/sites/peacemaker.un.org/files/IE%20GB_980410_Northern%20Ireland%20Agreement.pdf Belfast Agreement] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130501234234/http://peacemaker.un.org/sites/peacemaker.un.org/files/IE%20GB_980410_Northern%20Ireland%20Agreement.pdf |date=1 May 2013 }}, section: "Economic, Social and Cultural Issues", para. 5</ref>}}

Nationalists pointed to this to argue that the use of the Union Flag for official purposes should be restricted, or that the Irish tricolour should be flown alongside the British flag on government buildings. [[Sinn Féin]] ministers in the [[power sharing|power-sharing]] [[Northern Ireland Executive]] instructed that the Union Flag was not to fly from buildings operated by their respective departments.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/northern_ireland/773579.stm|title=BBC News - NORTHERN IRELAND - Tension over flag flying|website=News.bbc.co.uk|access-date=9 January 2018|archive-date=21 October 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221021054124/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/northern_ireland/773579.stm|url-status=live}}</ref> This power was removed from ministers by virtue of the Flag Regulations (Northern Ireland) Order 2000, mentioned above.

All signatories to the Good Friday Agreement also declare their acceptance of the "principle of consent" (i.e. that there will be no change to the constitutional position of Northern Ireland unless a majority votes for it), and Unionists argued that this provision amounts to recognising that the Union Flag is the only legitimate official flag in Northern Ireland.{{Citation needed|date=June 2007}} The problem was discussed in detail and various proposals made including suggestions for a new flag.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.democraticdialogue.org/working/flags.htm|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20050410183041/http://www.democraticdialogue.org/working/flags.htm|url-status=dead|title=''Flagging concern: the controversy over flags and emblems''|archivedate=10 April 2005}}</ref>

==Flag proposals==
===Haass talks===
In 2013, American diplomat [[Richard N. Haass]] chaired talks between the political parties in Northern Ireland dealing with, among other things, the issue of flags. The resulting draft proposals, which were not agreed to by the parties, included the idea of a new flag for Northern Ireland,<ref>{{Cite news |title=Haass proposes new body to investigate Troubles killings |url=https://www.irishtimes.com/news/ireland/irish-news/haass-proposes-new-body-to-investigate-troubles-killings-1.1629610 |access-date=19 September 2024 |newspaper=The Irish Times |language=en |archive-date=29 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201129202232/https://www.irishtimes.com/news/ireland/irish-news/haass-proposes-new-body-to-investigate-troubles-killings-1.1629610 |url-status=live }}</ref> and the possibility of a "circumscribed role for the sovereign flag of Ireland in conjunction with the Union flag."<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mulgrew |first=John |date=31 December 2013 |title=Final draft on dealing with Northern Ireland's past released after failure on agreement |url=https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/news/northern-ireland/final-draft-on-dealing-with-northern-irelands-past-released-after-failure-on-agreement/29878841.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231020205516/https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/news/northern-ireland/final-draft-on-dealing-with-northern-irelands-past-released-after-failure-on-agreement/29878841.html |archive-date=20 October 2023 |access-date=19 September 2024 |work=Belfast Telegraph |language=en-GB |issn=0307-1235}}</ref>

===Proposed "Civic Flag"===
In December 2021, the Commission on Flags, Identity, Culture and Tradition published its final report which included a recommendation that a new "Civic Flag for Northern Ireland" should be adopted and be flown at buildings of the Northern Ireland Executive, [[Northern Ireland Assembly]] and local district councils in Northern Ireland. The commissions suggested that the design for the new flag should incorporate expressions of Britishness and Irishness and should also represent the diversity of the community in Northern Ireland.<ref>{{Cite report |url=https://www.executiveoffice-ni.gov.uk/sites/default/files/publications/execoffice/commission-on-fict-final-report.pdf |title=Commission on Flags, Identity, Culture and Tradition: Final Report |date=1 December 2021 |access-date=19 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240228140340/https://www.executiveoffice-ni.gov.uk/sites/default/files/publications/execoffice/commission-on-fict-final-report.pdf |archive-date=28 February 2024}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=1 December 2021 |title=New Northern Ireland 'civic flag' considered as officials debate cultural future |url=https://www.itv.com/news/utv/2021-12-01/new-northern-ireland-civic-flag-considered-as-officials-debate-cultural-future |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240118024840/https://www.itv.com/news/utv/2021-12-01/new-northern-ireland-civic-flag-considered-as-officials-debate-cultural-future |archive-date=18 January 2024 |access-date=18 September 2024}}</ref><ref name="BBC News"/> The working group on flags, identity, culture and tradition last met in January 2022, prior to the collapse of the power sharing executive the following month. In November 2024, the Executive Office said that the Commission's report will be "considered as part of a review of its community relations strategy".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/clyveyz90y3o|title=Stormont's £800,000 flags report 'still on shelf' three years on|date=1 November 2024|website=BBC News|access-date=4 November 2024|archive-date=6 November 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241106124321/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/clyveyz90y3o|url-status=live}}</ref>

==International sport==
The [[Ulster Banner]] is used to represent the [[Northern Ireland at the Commonwealth Games|Northern Ireland team]] at the [[Commonwealth Games]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://thecgf.com/countries/northern-ireland|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190420033040/https://thecgf.com/countries/northern-ireland|url-status=dead|title=Northern Ireland &#124; Commonwealth Games Federation|archivedate=20 April 2019|website=thecgf.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/graphics/gallery/commonwealth06/opening/nireut.jpg|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20080406182956/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/graphics/gallery/commonwealth06/opening/nireut.jpg|url-status=dead|title=''Telegraph''|archivedate=6 April 2008}}</ref> to represent golfers on the [[PGA Tour]],<ref name="PGAProfile">{{cite web |title=Rory McIlroy PGA TOUR Profile - News, Stats, and Videos |url=https://www.pgatour.com/players/player.28237.rory-mcilroy.html |website=PGATour |publisher=[[PGA Tour]] |access-date=6 August 2018 |language=en |archive-date=5 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170805034513/http://www.pgatour.com/players/player.28237.html |url-status=live }}</ref> and by [[FIFA]] to represent the [[Northern Ireland national football team]].<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20070917110313/http://fifa.com/associations/association=nir/countryInfo.html Northern Ireland: Country information], FIFA.com</ref>

The [[International Volleyball Federation]] uses the Saint Patrick's Saltire to represent the Northern Ireland national volleyball team.<ref>{{Cite web | title=Federations {{!}} InsideCEV | url=https://inside.cev.eu/institutions/federations/ | access-date=2026-06-28 | website=inside.cev.eu | archive-date=16 April 2026 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260416212317/https://inside.cev.eu/institutions/federations/ | url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | title=Rankings {{!}} CEV | url=https://www.cev.eu/national-team/rankings/ | access-date=2026-06-28 | website=www.cev.eu | archive-date=7 December 2021 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211207160213/https://www.cev.eu/national-team/rankings/ | url-status=live }}</ref>

In November 2024, the [[Northern Ireland Commonwealth Games Council]] (NICGC) discussed the possibility of using its own flag at the [[Commonwealth Games]] if a new "Civic Flag" proposed by the Commission on Flags, Identity, Culture and Traditions is not in place before the [[2026 Commonwealth Games]].<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2024/11/26/ireland-commonwealth-games-ditch-offensive-ulster-banner/ | title=Ulster banner may be dropped from Commonwealth Games | newspaper=The Telegraph | last1=Kelly | first1=Kieran | archive-date=8 December 2024 | access-date=7 December 2024 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241208084648/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2024/11/26/ireland-commonwealth-games-ditch-offensive-ulster-banner/ | url-status=live }}</ref> The proposal to replace the [[Ulster Banner]] was met with criticism from politicians and activists. Following the announcement, NICGC chief executive Conal Heatley reported receiving "threatening and abusive language on social media", prompting the temporary closure of its office while a security assessment was undertaken.<ref>{{Cite web | title=NI Commonwealth Games office closed amid threats over flag change proposal {{!}} UTV {{!}} ITV News | url=https://www.itv.com/news/utv/2024-11-27/ni-commonwealth-games-office-closed-amid-threats-over-flag-change-proposal | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241207222838/https://www.itv.com/news/utv/2024-11-27/ni-commonwealth-games-office-closed-amid-threats-over-flag-change-proposal | archive-date=2024-12-07 | access-date=2026-06-28}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-11-29 |title=NI Commonwealth Games chief Conal Heatley raises safety concerns |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/czxrj1vrxn8o |access-date=2025-03-29 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241129040818/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/czxrj1vrxn8o |archive-date=29 November 2024 }}</ref>
